WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.820
C++ programs often manage resources beyond heap memory.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:19.160
File handles, sockets, operating system handles, library contexts, cryptographic contexts, GPU resources, memory-mapped files, pools, and arenas can all leak if ownership is unclear.

3
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.680
Resource ownership should be represented in types, usually through RAII wrappers.

4
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:28.900
A wrapper can define how the resource is acquired, moved, released, and inspected.

5
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.960
It can also prevent accidental copying when copying would duplicate ownership incorrectly.

6
00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:37.440
Custom deleters must match cleanup contracts.

7
00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:43.460
A resource created by one library function may require a specific release function.

8
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:47.400
A mismatch can leak the resource or release it incorrectly.

9
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:53.180
Reviewers should be able to connect creation and cleanup without tracing unrelated code.

10
00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:59.940
Pools and arenas can be safe and useful, but they require clear reset rules.

11
00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:03.960
A pool reset may invalidate many objects at once.

12
00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:07.980
An arena may retain memory until a larger scope ends.

13
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.280
These lifetime choices must be documented so callers do not assume ordinary individual cleanup.

14
00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:17.480
Specialized allocators require documentation and review.

15
00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:27.500
State who owns the allocator, when memory is released, whether the allocator is thread-safe, what happens when it is exhausted, and how usage is measured.

16
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.200
Performance optimization should not hide lifecycle responsibility.

17
00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:34.760
A good wrapper makes the correct cleanup path ordinary.

18
00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:43.180
It should be movable when transfer is allowed, noncopyable when ownership must remain unique, and small enough that teams will use it consistently.
